Priest Akka Chota Srey Ly was a Thera who built a shrine to Buddhism. He never had a wife and had a veneration in Buddhism from a young age until his last breath between the wars.
He was an intellectual monk with a high level of knowledge in Buddhism, open-minded, impartial, and non-judgmental.
After graduating, he spent most of his time working at the pagoda, working as a priest and participating in general social activities. He loved to study, even in his old age. He struggled to give the monks in the whole pagoda a chance to go to school.
He was a monk who did not have much order, respected other monks, did not like pride and did not like to appear in society.
In the pagoda, he is a good teacher and a role model who is respected by the monks in the pagoda and the Buddhists at the foot of the pagoda.
In addition to practicing discipline, he spent time with the monks and the environment in the pagoda, especially in matters of hygiene and aesthetics.
He used his own money received from the Buddhists to build, renovate and garden the entire pagoda. He often hired poor Buddhists to help clean the pagoda just to make a living.
All he wanted was peace in the pagoda and the community in which he lived, and his last breath left in peace. Khmer civilization expert: shed tears but do not cry, show real will: sharp and beautiful eyes
It means that the cries of Cambodian children at that time were expressed from the heart due to pain and despair, tears flowed because they did not know what rules could save their lives, cried and cried until they became a sea. The earth is covered with blood.
Mr. Rath Sandab continued that when it comes to crying, it is a conversation to express sympathy, to remember a situation or event that happened in the past that can not be forgotten. In this sense, the tears of Samdech Techo's 21st grandson reflect the tragic events in the history of Cambodia during the Killing Fields regime, because at that time the world did not pay attention or care about water. The eyes of Cambodian children.
Not only that, but it also reflects the cruelty of the world without mercy on Cambodian children who have suffered in the past. Therefore, for the tears that flowed down the cheeks but did not cry, the 21st grandson of Samdech Techo now can show patience, depression and shed tears but do not cry is to maintain the dignity of Cambodian children.
Thus, for the tears but not crying of the grandson, Samdech Techo showed the true will and the sharp and beautiful eyes of a very strong child, not crying. Although the child's substance has been described as crying, it can alleviate some of the pain.
