BEIJING (Reuters) - Beijing on Sunday announced the downgrade of its diplomatic ties with Lithuania to show strong opposition to the government of Vilnius allowing Taiwan to set up a representative office there. This was released by Reuters on Sunday, November 21, 2021.
"Lithuania ignores the position of the Chinese government, does not care about the overall situation of bilateral relations and does not adhere to the standards of international relations by allowing Taiwan to set up a representative office," the Chinese Foreign Ministry said in a statement. In their own country. "Beijing urges Lithuania to rectify this mistake as soon as possible."
In August 2021, China demanded that Lithuania recall its ambassador to Beijing, and that Beijing would recall its representative in Vilnius after Taiwan's announcement. That its office in Lithuania will be called the Taiwan Representative Office. Other Taiwan offices in Europe and the United States use the name Taipei, not the Taiwanese name.
