Trade between China and Africa rose 38.2 percent in the first nine months of this year to 185.2 billion yuan, according to Xinhua, citing Chinese Commerce Minister Qian Keming. Millions of dollars, the highest level ever for the same period.
Qian said China's direct investment in Africa reached $ 2.59 billion from January to September, up 9.9 percent from a year earlier. The growth rate was 3 percentage points higher than China's overall overseas investment. He added that the growth rate was higher than before the Kovid-19 crisis in the same period in 2019.
Chinese enterprises signed new contracts worth $ 53.5 billion in Africa during the period, up 22.2 percent from a year earlier, with total revenue of 26.9 percent. Billion dollars, up 11.6 percent from the same period last year.
At the end of November, the 8th Ministerial Conference of the China-Africa Cooperation Forum (FOCAC) will be held in Senegal, Xinhua reported.
China has maintained its position as Africa's largest trading partner for 12 consecutive years. Bilateral trade will reach $ 187 billion by 2020 According to the Xinhua news agency.
