South Korea reduces Kovid-19 vaccination interval, boosts aging
South Korea, which is currently easing restrictions under the COVID-19 study plan, has vaccinated more than 90 percent of the adult population. But at the same time, cases of infection among those who have been vaccinated have been reported, especially among the elderly.
That is why the South Korean Center for Infectious Disease Control and Prevention (KDCA) has decided to reduce the vaccination interval from six months to six months for the elderly, those who live or work in nursing homes and the elderly. Other vulnerabilities. People in their 50s, soldiers, police and firefighters can receive a booster dose within five months of receiving their second dose.
