Ministry of Public Works calls on citizens and road users to pay attention to traffic accidents such as Kovid 19
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Public Works and Transport (MPWT) has called on residents, especially road users, to pay attention to traffic accidents, such as the 199 Kovid epidemic, in order to reduce road deaths in the country. . This is according to Ms. Mon Manvy, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and Secretary General of the National Road Safety Committee.
She stated in the traffic safety awareness promotion to passengers on National Road No. 1 at the point of Korki Market on the morning of November 17, 2021 that traffic accidents are not vaccinated like Kovid 19, so people and road users. High attention should be paid to reducing road deaths in Cambodia as Kovid 19 has steadily reduced.
She said that on the occasion of the upcoming Water Festival to reduce traffic accidents, the National Road Safety Committee will work to promote traffic safety throughout the country.
"We do not plan to distribute any hats for the Water Festival, but we try to spread the word on the streets," she said. "And the distribution will follow the possibility that so far has distributed about 1,000 hats in each province, which across the country has distributed more than 20,000 hats."
She also said that the promotion of traffic safety due to the reopening of the country by the Royal Government after the 19-year-old Kovid disease was alleviated also caused traffic congestion.
On the occasion of the Water Festival, the Secretary of State called on motorists, cars, bicycles and pedestrians to be careful. For driving, do not drive faster than the speed limit, in which case it is necessary to understand the priority rights as well. "Please understand which way is left, which way is right, and we must always listen, do not turn and overtake in a dangerous situation, and do not drink and drive."
The Secretary of State also emphasized: "Beware of Kovid 19! Remember, when saying Kovid, think of an accident. Covet and traffic go together because they die the same way. "Kovid has a vaccine, but traffic is not a vaccine, only we are careful when traveling on the road."
At the same time, she also said that in 2022, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport has more than 10,000 helmets left, and the Ministry has instructed all localities to promote traffic safety by distributing helmets. Safety as possible as well.
She said that if you do not wear a safety helmet, the police will pay 60,000 riel, which is worth about 60,000 riel, and wear a helmet and obey the law. Officials from the Ministry of Public Works also said that traffic safety awareness campaigns will be carried out throughout the capital and in schools, streets and markets.
So far, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport has distributed nearly 90,000 traffic helmets, of which 100,000 are planned, according to the official.
Ministry of Planning issues press release on poverty line and poverty rate in Cambodia 2019-2020
Recently, the Royal Government of Cambodia has set a new poverty line using data from the Cambodia Socio-Economic Survey 2019-2020. The national poverty line for Cambodia is 10,951 riels per person per day.
Based on the national poverty line, the regional poverty line is as follows:
According to the press release, in rural areas, about 17.8% of the total population lived below this line in 2019-2020, with the poverty rate at:
This poverty line has been used to measure poverty in Cambodia to date (adjusted for inflation in each year).
The National Working Group on Measuring Poverty in Cambodia, under the leadership of the Ministry of Planning and in close collaboration with the World Bank, began reviewing and revising its approach to measuring poverty in Cambodia in 2017.
"This review indicates that the rapid development of the Cambodian economy over the past decade has led to significant changes in consumer spending, such as higher spending on Cambodia," the statement said. Education, health, housing, personal telephones and internet to meet basic needs, including the cost of using the poor.
This revised approach focuses on two main points: (a) creating a new total utilization cost that incorporates the estimated cost of rent and the estimated cost of using durable materials; and (b) Implement a cost-based approach to basic needs and common baskets to define the poverty line.
