China's coal output in October hit record high since 2015 (Video inside) China's coal output in October peaked in March 2015 comes after Beijing approved the expansion of coal mines for the highest price habits and increased supply. According to Reuters.
Reuters reported that according to the National Bureau of Statistics, the world's largest producer and consumer of coal produced 357.09 million tonnes last month, up from 334.1 million tonnes in September.
Coal output in the first 10 months of this year was 3.3 billion tons, up 4 percent from the same period last year.
China's National Development and Reform Commission (NDRC) said last month that since July, China has approved the expansion of more than 153 coal mines with a capacity of 55 million tons of fish. The fourth gold.
The Chinese government, which is also trying to end power shortages, has also banned local administrations from shutting down unlicensed coal mines and urged the immediate resumption of mines that were shut down in the past. That has already fixed the problem.
Daily coal production reached a record high of 12.05 million tonnes after falling last week by more than 1 million tonnes, the NDRC, China's top planning body, said last week.
Reuters reports that the price of coal in the spot market immediately fell by about half from a month ago to $ 156.7 a tonne in the northern port of China due to intervention measures. Many issued by the Beijing government.
