Crude oil prices fall due to declining demand and increased supply expectations
SINGAPORE: Crude oil prices fell today amid expectations of an increase in supply, as higher energy costs and an increase in Kovid-19 disease are also seen as a burden. On demand as well. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports that the oil market has been declining in the last three weeks due to the strength of the dollar and the expectation of President Joe Biden's government to release oil from US strategic oil stocks to lower oil prices.
"The White House has been debating how to deal with higher inflation, US oil companies this week opened more oil and gas wells," ANZ analysts said in a report. "Three more weeks in a row as crude prices rose to near seven-year highs pushed those companies back to production."
Meanwhile, OPEC last week cut its global oil demand forecast for the fourth quarter by 330,000 barrels a day from last month's forecast as higher energy prices hampered the recovery. Economic recovery from the Kovid-19 pandemic. According to Reuters.
US, Japan begin talks on steel, aluminum tariffs
The United States has said it has opened talks with Japan aimed at reducing tariffs on US steel and aluminum imports under Donald Trump. According to the AFP news agency.
The talks come after Washington reached an agreement on the same issue with the European Union last week.
The concerns raised by both of them are aimed at damaging the steel and aluminum markets caused by oversupply in China, driven by China.
"These discussions present an opportunity to raise standards for addressing common concerns, including climate change, and forcing countries such as China to support and implement non-market-oriented policies responsibly," they added. "
The two senior US officials said that the damage to the market caused by overcapacity caused by non-market factors, as a large part caused by China, poses a serious threat to the steel industry. Thep and US US-based market aluminum.
Her first official visit to Asia will also be to Malaysia and Singapore, where she will meet with officials from Australia and New Zealand, the AFP news agency reported.
In June 2018, Trump imposed a 25% tariff on steel and 10% on aluminum imports from a number of countries, including the European Union and Japan.
At the time, the former president claimed that the tax was based on national security, a claim denied by his critics.
