Chinese PM vows to provide more vaccines to Cambodia to help prevent Kovid 19
(Phnom Penh): Chinese Premier Li Keqiang has pledged to provide the Cambodian government with an additional 19 vaccines against measles.
Mr. Ly Ker Chheang's promise was made during a special video conference with Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen on Monday afternoon, November 15, 2021.
Mr. Eang Sophalleth, Samdech Techo Hun Sen's personal assistant, emphasized that in this meeting, Mr. Ly Ker Chheang promised to continue to help Cambodia in the fight against Kovid 19 by providing vaccines to help prevent Kovid 19.
"In the future, the government and the people will continue to provide vaccines to 19 Kovid doctors and other medical equipment to Cambodia," Li Keqiang said. This is according to Mr. Eang Sophalleth.
Cambodia has received more than 30 million doses of 19 smallpox vaccines to date, with China providing the largest number of vaccines to Cambodia. In addition, there is the World Health Organization through the COCAC mechanism, Japan, the United Kingdom and the United States through CAVAC.
China provides 19 more Kovid vaccines than any other country in the world, while most of the vaccines received by developing countries come from China. So far, China has provided more than 1.7 billion doses of the vaccine to more than 110 countries and international organizations, aiming to reach 2 billion doses by the end of this year. China continues to provide scholarships to 500 more Cambodian students for the 2022-2023 academic year
(Phnom Penh): During a special video discussion meeting between Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Li Keqiang, Prime Minister of the People's Republic of China, on the afternoon of November 15, Prime Minister of the People's Republic of China Li Keqiang Pledge to continue to provide scholarships to 500 more Cambodian students for the academic year 2022-2023.
Mr. Eang Sophalleth, Samdech Techo Hun Sen's personal assistant, emphasized after the meeting between the Prime Ministers of the two countries that the additional 500 scholarships are for 5 years and China is examining the possibility of opening for Cambodian students to continue. Study in China again soon.
"His Excellency Li Keqiang also confirmed that China will continue to provide scholarships to 500 Cambodian students for the 2022-2023 academic year for an additional five years. The Chinese government is also reviewing the opening of Cambodian students to resume direct education in China in the near future.
According to the report of the Cambodian Students Association in China, there are 1,707 Cambodian students studying in China (1,060 males and 647 females), including 37 doctoral students, 327 masters, and 1081 undergraduates. 84 associate degrees and 178 language classes.
According to the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, from 1980-2020, a total of 19546 Cambodian students (4,228 females) received scholarships to study in 32 other countries. Cambodia-China free trade agreement to enter into force in January next year, Chinese PM wants to see all Cambodian agricultural products can be exported directly to China without going through other countries
(Phnom Penh): Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Chinese Premier Li Keqiang have announced that a free trade agreement between Cambodia and China will enter into force in January 2022.
This announcement was made during a meeting between Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Mr. Ly Ker Chheang via video on Monday afternoon, November 15, 2021.
A high-value free trade agreement to boost trade between Cambodia and China was signed on October 12, 2020, but it has not yet been implemented, subject to legal requirements. According to each country, as well as the approval of the parliamentary bodies of both countries.
Mr. Eang Sophalleth, Samdech Techo Hun Sen's personal assistant, confirmed to the media after the meeting that Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Mr. Ly Ker Chheang discussed the focus on trade between the two countries and the trade agreement. This free, too. In terms of trade, the Prime Ministers of Cambodia and China agreed to boost the two countries' trade volume to 10 billion US dollars.
In addition, Samdech Techo and Mr. Li Keqiang agreed that the free trade agreement between the two countries will enter into force and start implementing trade between the two countries in January 2022.
Mr. Eang Sophalleth stated that "Samdech Techo and HE Ly Ker Chheang have agreed to boost trade between the two countries to 10 billion dollars per year and also agreed to enter into force. "Free trade agreement between Cambodia and China in January."
In relation to this trade, Li Keqiang called for discussions on China's rice and agricultural import quotas. For the Chinese Prime Minister, he wants to see Cambodia import all kinds of agricultural products directly to China to avoid passing through other countries.
A free trade agreement (FTA) is a mutual tax exemption agreement between one country and another on an agreed product. The FTA is not only a product tax exemption agreement, but covers many areas, including services, investment, trade facilitation, and the removal of non-tariff barriers.
For the free trade agreement between Cambodia and China, it is claimed that Cambodia will import more than 340 products, 95 percent of which will be taxed to 0 percent, and the remaining 5 percent will be down within 10 years or next year. To 0%.
According to Sok Sopheak, most of Cambodia's duty-free goods to China are chemical minerals for industrial use, as well as imported plastics for assembly and export.
Please note that China is the largest source of foreign direct investment and also Cambodia's most important trading partner. In the context of the 19 19 outbreak of Kovid, trade volume between Cambodia and China continued to grow significantly.
Mr. Sok Sopheak once said that in the first six months of 2021, the trade volume between Cambodia and China reached nearly 5 billion US dollars, an increase of 31 percent.
In general, Cambodia exports to China garments, shoes, leather, fur, machinery, pistons, rice, rubber, bananas, cassava, cashew nuts, shoes, suitcases, etc.
As of November 15, 2021, Cambodia has received a total of 30 million doses of Kovid 19 vaccine to give to the people, of which China has supplied the most vaccines to Cambodia.
In addition, there is the World Health Organization through the CAVAC mechanism, Japan, the United Kingdom and the United States through CAVAC.
The presence of the Chinese Kovid 19 vaccine in Cambodia clearly demonstrates the spirit of steel friendship between China and Cambodia, the spirit of helping each other through adversity. In particular, it shows the strong Chinese spirit of participation in helping to fight Kovid 19 in Cambodia.
