Indian Supreme Court calls for 'two-day shutdown of New Delhi' due to rising pollution
The Indian court's request was made during a hearing on a case of air pollution in the capital. Air pollution across New Delhi is "severe" and will continue to rise for the next two to three days, the court said. That is why the court asked the federal government to make an immediate decision to improve air quality.
"We have all seen the situation worse because even in our homes we have to wear masks," the court said. In the past 10 days, heavy smog has been covering the entire city.
According to the Indian Supreme Court, farmers' actions alone cannot be blamed for rising pollution levels due to the seasonal burning of their fields. Authorities have to deal with other causes, including vehicle pollution, dust and industrial pollution. The court has asked the federal government to convene an emergency meeting with the participation of all stakeholders and hold a follow-up hearing next Monday.
