(Phnom Penh): Cambodia and the Republic of Korea have agreed to expand cooperation to attract more tourists from Korea to visit Cambodia, while Cambodia is also ready to reopen international tourists within the strategic framework of open vaccination and tourism. To expand the tourism development of the two countries.
On that occasion, Minister Thong Khon said that under the direct guidance and guidance of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Cambodia has achieved more than 80% vaccination of people across the country. The results of vaccination, public education, and community resilience are a warm foundation for a step-by-step, high-responsibility socio-economic opening.
The Minister said that Cambodia plans to open to international tourists in Sihanoukville, Koh Rong and Darasakor areas of Koh Kong province from November 30, 2021 and Siem Reap Angkor will reopen in January 2020. Then we will continue to open Phnom Penh and all over the country.
The Ambassador of the Republic of Korea supported the initiative to establish a major event in 2022 to celebrate the 25th anniversary of Cambodia-Korea diplomatic ties to attract Korean tourists and investment to Cambodia. He also invited the Ministry of Tourism to participate in major international events in the bilateral framework as well as the Mekong-Korea framework.
In response to Cambodia's request, the Government of the Republic of Korea will consider promoting the provision of additional public toilets towards the promotion of cleanliness and hygiene in the 2023 SEA Games and continue to organize more tourist destinations, especially in the Northeast.
The Korean side will encourage the participation of Korean athletes in the Cambodian sailing program in December 2021. At the same time, the Korean side is ready to participate in the ASEAN Plus Three Tourism Ministers' Meeting on the occasion of ASEAN Tourism 2022 (ATF 2022) from January 16-22, 2022.
At the invitation of Cambodia, the Korean side will also promote its participation in the General Assembly of the Most Beautiful Beach Club in the World and the Sea Festival at the end of 2022.
The Embassy of the Republic of Korea will also help promote Cambodia's tourism potential as well as good cooperation with Cambodia to the Korean people through the publication of articles.
But apart from the agreement and the photo opportunity at the meeting, what are the key points that countries need to address to address climate change? Here are some key points that countries should consider to restore the situation from worsening:
* Keep fossil fuels in the ground
Burning fossil fuels such as oil, gas, and especially coal often emits carbon dioxide (CO2) into the atmosphere, absorbing heat and raising global temperatures. It is a problem to be solved at the government level if the temperature rise is set to 1.5 degrees Celsius, which is considered by scientists to be a gateway to dangerous climate change.
However, many major coal-dependent countries, such as Australia, the United States, China and India, refused to sign an agreement on the issue at COP26.
* Prevent methane emissions
Large amounts of methane are released from "combustion" during the extraction of natural gas during extraction, and such activities can be stopped by finding alternative technical solutions. Finding better ways to dispose of waste is also important, as landfills are a major source of methane emissions.
Electricity and heat generation have contributed significantly to global emissions than other economic development sectors. The transformation of the global energy system from a primary reliance on fossil fuels to the use of clean energy technology known as decarbonisation is crucial for achieving the current climate goals.
Wind and solar will be the main sources of energy by 2050 if the countries are committed to achieving this common goal. However, there are also many obstacles.
Less wind also means less electricity, but better energy-saving technology (batteries) today can help us save energy and use it for natural energy. Enough.
* Abandon gasoline and diesel
We will also have to change the way we power the vehicles we use to travel on land and sea. Abandonment of gasoline and diesel vehicles and switching to electric vehicles will be important.
Trucks and buses can run on hydrogen fuel, which is produced with a high degree of renewable energy in mind. Scientists are working on new and cleaner fuels for aircraft, though some campaigners are urging people to reduce the number of flights they have to fly.
* Planting more trees
* Eliminate greenhouse gases from the air
Another new method is the capture and storage of carbon deep in the soil, which is usually carried out in coal-fired power plants. But the technology is expensive and controversial because critics see it as inseparable from the use of fossil fuels such as coal.
* Provide financial assistance to poor countries
According to experts, poor countries need financial assistance to help them turn to "clean energy". To achieve this, the United States, Britain and the European Union have pledged $ 8.5 billion in aid to South Africa to gradually stop using coal.
