CMA: Decrease in Credit Restructuring Requests as a Sign of Cambodia's Economic Recovery
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kaing Tong Ngy, Spokesman of the Cambodian Microfinance Association (CMA) said that in the last 10 weeks, from August to October 2021, the application for reorganization of customers has Between 100-500 people per week. This sign of decline is indicative of a return to the Cambodian economy.
He added that the decline could be attributed to a significant reduction in the impact of Kovid 19 and that the number of Kovid 19 victims was declining as well, which is a good sign. One shows that the Cambodian economy is gradually recovering.
He added that despite the improved situation, the MFI will continue to monitor various trends to prevent any possible impact.
"The credit restructuring policy will expire by the end of 2021, but the financial sector as a whole is studying with relevant institutions to extend the policy as some clients are still affected," he said.
