On the morning of November 12, 2021, the Court of Appeal sentenced Rong Chhun to two years in prison, but served only 15 months and 11 days, with the remaining 8 months and 19 days suspended. Separately, two activists, Mr. Tol Nimol and Ms. Saknika, were also tried and released by the court.
* Please note: In the case of Mr. Tol Nimol was released one case, the other case, according to a senior official of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court.
In addition to the three above, two other youths, Hun Vannak and Chhoeun Daravy, were also released by the court after serving their sentences. In addition, the court also released Suong Sophorn and Pen Chansongkram.
Rong Chhun was sentenced by the Phnom Penh Municipal Court to two years in prison on August 18, 2021 for inciting trouble on the Cambodia-Vietnam border in Tbong Khmum province last year. Mr. Suong Sophorn, Mr. Pen Chansangkram, Mr. Tol Nimol and Ms. Saknika, who protested for the release of Mr. Rong Chhun, were arrested last year.
Rong Chhun was charged with "inciting to cause social security unrest" after being arrested on July 31, 2020 after committing a crime of inciting Chaos during the visit to the Cambodian-Vietnamese border in Tbong Khmum province. While visiting the provincial border, Mr. Rong Chhun issued a statement to the public that the new border post between Cambodia and Vietnam had entered Cambodian territory about 500 meters.
Immediately after the allegations, the Cambodian Joint Border Affairs Committee dismissed Rong Chhun's statement as false and exaggerated. The Joint Committee on Border Affairs considers Rong Chhun's broadcast to be malicious in order to mislead national and international public by making baseless allegations about the Royal Government's border work.
