Arrests a Broker and 14 Workers for Illegal Crossing to Thailand
(Banteay Meanchey): Cambodian border police cracked down on illegal smuggling to Thailand, detaining one of two brokers and 14 male and female workers in two vehicles.
The operation to crack down on illegal cross-border crime took place at 11 pm on November 10, 2021 in Kilometer 4 Village, Sangkat Phsar Kandal, Poipet City, Banteay Meanchey Province.
Brigadier General Ou Sophal, Deputy Commissioner for Border Planning of the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Police, said that in accordance with the order of Brigadier General Sithi Loh, the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Police Commissioner, he led a search force to seize two vehicles.
The two vehicles, after transporting the workers from Kilometer 4 village to Malai district, drove to the corner of Khla Ngap road, Malai district, Banteay Meanchey province. Police arrested a 28-year-old man named Chhum Saphon, along with 14 workers, who confiscated a Camry car carrying illegal immigrants.
The 14 workers were sent to the Tuol Pongro administrative post for questioning.
Currently, both the broker and the car, including all workers, have been sent to the Malai District Police Inspectorate for legal action. Evergrande escapes debt default again, but sector debt concerns remain
Evergrande, the world's most indebted real estate developer, has been struggling to pay off its debts from one deadline to another in recent weeks, Reuters reported. The company has more than $ 300 billion in debt, of which $ 19 billion is in international bonds.
The debt settlement comes after the end of a 30-day grace period that ended on Wednesday, as in the other two cases of late repayment of foreign debt owed late in September. And the grace period ended in October.
Failure to repay any debt will result in a formal failure for this company and push for the implementation of cross-sectional debt regulation (failure of one debt to another) for another company's dollar-denominated bonds. Evergrande, which will only exacerbate the debt crisis in the world's second-largest economy, has shaken global markets.
Evergrande did not respond to a request for comment from Reuters regarding the payment of interest on the company's final bonds.
Although the developer was able to escape the debt failure again, problems in the real estate sector showed no signs of abating, with the debt wall always on the road ahead.
Evergrande has to pay a total of more than $ 255 million in interest due on December 28. The company is under pressure from other creditors in the country, and a lack of funding has hampered hundreds of its housing development projects. According to Reuters.
