According to a press release from the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, in accordance with the Memorandum of Understanding between the Royal Government of Cambodia and the United States Government, four Cambodian artifacts will be returned to Cambodia in the near future.
These four Khmer artifacts include:
1: Sculpture of Pracha Baramita, sandstone of the Angkorian period, Bayon style, late 12th-early 13th century. According to eyewitnesses, the statue was found in a rice field near an ancient temple in Takeo province not far from Tonle Bati.
2: Pre-Angkorian sandstone sandstone sculpture, 7th-8th centuries. According to witnesses, the statue was stolen from a temple on Mount Kulen.
3: Bronze bell, prehistoric period. According to eyewitness accounts, the bell was one of 12 bronze objects (arranged in a circle around a cornerstone) found in an area of Pursat-Battambang province.
Dr. Pheung Sakona, Minister of Culture and Fine Arts, stated that we are all excited to continue to welcome these precious treasures back to Cambodia and are proud of our cooperation with the United States Government.
She continued that the results obtained in this series are because Cambodia is completely peaceful through the win-win policy of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, providing an opportunity for the return of the souls of Cambodian ancestors who have drifted away. From my homeland for many years during the war.
The Minister appealed to museums and private collectors who hold Khmer cultural treasures to contact the Ministry to share relevant documents and voluntarily return those cultural treasures to Cambodia.
The ministry is continuing to gather evidence of the smuggling and illegal possession of Cambodian cultural property.
In addition, the Minister stressed the value of cooperation and joint efforts of the two countries in protecting the heritage, cultural heritage and national identity for the benefit of the Cambodian people and humanity in general.
