Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon encourages more Australian companies to invest in Cambodia as both countries recover from the same Kovid 19 pandemic
(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia, has encouraged more Australian companies to invest in Cambodia as the two countries are recovering from the Kovid 19 pandemic. Each. This is according to a press release from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia, obtained by Fresh News.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon made the remarks during a bilateral meeting with Australian Minister of Foreign Affairs and Minister of Women's Affairs Mary Phen during her two-day official visit to Cambodia on July 7 and 8. November 2021 ago.
A statement from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia on the outcome of the visit of Ms. Mariss Phen on November 9, 2021 stated: "HE Deputy Prime Minister encourages more Australian companies to invest in Cambodia while Cambodia "A new investment law has just been passed that will provide a better investment climate."
"In response, Ms. Penn Pan stressed that while Cambodia and Australia are recovering from the same epidemic, there are many opportunities for both countries to work together to boost trade and investment." This is according to the press release of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
Australia and Cambodia began diplomatic ties in 1952. Trade relations between Australia and Cambodia are growing rapidly. In 2020, similar goods traded between the two countries totaled $ 432.4 million, up 21.6 percent from 2019. Australian imports to Cambodia in 2020 totaled $ 121.4 million, up 43.5 percent from 2019.
At the same bilateral meeting, Ms. Pan also reiterated Australia's commitment to continue to assist and cooperate with Cambodia in areas such as economic development, demining, agriculture, health and education, especially Continue to provide scholarships to Cambodian students every year under the Australian Scholarship Program and the Mekong-Australia Partnership Program.
The Australian Foreign Minister made the remarks during a bilateral meeting with Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia, during a two-day official visit to Cambodia on November 7 and 8, 2021. Go here.
A press release from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia on the outcome of the visit of Ms. Mariss Phen on November 9, 2021 stated that: For cooperation under the framework of the Mekong-Australia Partnership, HE Deputy Prime Minister expressed his appreciation. Praise Australia's vision for the Greater Mekong Subregion by promoting human resource development, economic resilience, trade and investment, and strengthening the internet and vital energy.
The statement added: "Ms Marie Penn reaffirmed her continued support and assistance under this framework amounting to AUD 232 million (approximately US $ 168 million) for four years to assist "To rebuild the economy after the Kovid-19 crisis and improve economic and environmental resilience in the region."
