(Phnom Penh): Mr. Chea Vandeth, Minister of Posts and Telecommunications, said that the 19 Kovid epidemic has disrupted the way of life, business and communication. However, it has allowed us to accelerate the digital transformation and adapt ourselves to the digital economy as a new growth model.
He said that taking into account the challenges and opportunities recently, the Royal Government of Cambodia has approved the policy framework on digital economy and society 2021-2035. Through a long-term vision, building a digital economy and society will focus primarily on building the foundation for improving technology operations and change among key stakeholders such as government, citizens and business.
"The Ministry is committed to collaborating with stakeholders, especially the private sector, in the digital realm and promoting financing for the Cambodian people through reliable technology and fast digital structures," he added.
Together with the digital transformation, the Ministry of Posts and Telecommunications plans to reduce the use of paper in the administrative process starting January 1, 2022. The IT team of the Ministry has worked on 20 digital projects that will contribute to more efficient and effective work.
"In the telecommunications sector, MPTC has set up five working groups focused on improving quality services and backbone networks across the country, as well as eight other working groups to strengthen telecommunications and internet services in all cities. "Our long-term strategy also ensures network connectivity for schools and health centers at the commune level across the country."
Naikwali Shah Momim, National Emergency Operations Coordinator for the Polio Vaccination Campaign of the Afghan Ministry of Health, told Reuters that the campaign started in some parts of the country on Monday, but there were obstacles. Many revolve around the lack of trained staff.
The World Health Organization (WHO) says a campaign aimed at vaccinating more than 3 million children in Afghanistan against polio has been supported by the Taliban, which will allow our staff to be vaccinated. To children in previously inaccessible areas.
Neighboring Afghanistan and Pakistan are the last countries in the world to have polio, an incurable disease and a contagious water-borne disease that can cause disability in children. Small.
The disease is eradicated almost worldwide through vaccination. But security concerns, inaccessibility to large-scale displacement and suspicions of outside interference have prevented large-scale vaccinations in Afghanistan and parts of Pakistan.
