(Sydney): Sydney Airport announced today that it has agreed to a $ 17 billion bid by a group of Australian investors, just days after the launch of international travel. Repeat. According to the AFP news agency.
"Today's announcement marks the end of months of negotiations between all parties," the AFP news agency quoted the chairman of Sydney Airport Operations as saying. "The Sydney Airport Council believes this result reflects a reasonable long-term cost for the airport."
The announcement comes as Australia's international border has partially reopened after being closed for nearly 600 days due to the Kovid-19 outbreak.
Traveled vaccinated Australians through Sydney and Melbourne airports can now travel back and forth without a permit or without an official waiver as the measure is being considered. Seen as the country's first step in returning to travel slowly.
The sale of the airport is subject to a number of other conditions, including an assessment by an independent expert. Shareholders are expected to consider the deal at a meeting in the first quarter of 2022, which requires the approval of a majority of 75% of shareholders to approve the sale. According to the AFP news agency.
