"Do not forget those who want to call for the Paris Agreement to cover you, you are calling foreigners to control Cambodia again," Hun Sen said.
An agreement on a common political solution to the Cambodian conflict, commonly known as the "Paris Peace Accords", was established on October 23, 1991, and is now 30 years old.
The agreement established the United Nations Transitional Authority (UNTAC), fulfilling its mission from 1992 to 1993 to restore the nation through the holding of Cambodia's first general elections in May 1993.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen reminded that UNTAC started to manage Cambodia from the Paris Agreement until the Constituent Assembly became the National Assembly.
"The Constitution is now the King, why would he want to be called back in control if you are not an animal, and on the other hand, UNTAC has finished its duties and the United Nations has finished its duties," he said. .
UNTAC ended its mandate after Cambodia held its first elections and established the 1993 Constitution, the nation's supreme law. The Constitution states that the King of Cambodia reigns but does not rule. The King is the head of state for life. The King cannot be abused by anyone.
"If you want to call a foreigner to control you, you face the law, and if you want the Paris Agreement to return, you have to pull out the four armed forces," he added. "But now only a small number of signatories to the Paris Agreement."
