North Korea conducts artillery drill after series of missile tests
SEOUL: North Korea just held artillery drills this weekend as part of efforts to strengthen its defense capabilities, state media said Sunday. This was released by Reuters on Sunday, November 7, 2021.
The KCNA news agency reported that the artillery drill was held on Saturday this weekend, but was not attended by North Korean leader Kim Jong Un. The exercise comes after a series of missile tests by North Korea in recent months.
Pyongyang has repeatedly criticized what it considers "double standards" because its military actions have often been criticized internationally, even if they were aimed at boosting defense capabilities. The United States has never been criticized internationally.
According to analysts, North Korea is trying to normalize its defense activities with the aim of eventually gaining international recognition for its nuclear weapons as well as its ballistic missiles. Currently under UN Security Council sanctions.
Most North Korean artillery is deployed near the border with South Korea, where they are capable of shelling as far as Seoul, while the South Korean capital is not far from the border. No border.
