(Phnom Penh): Ms. Mary Pann, Minister of Foreign Affairs and Minister of Women's Affairs of Australia, arrived at Phnom Penh International Airport this Sunday, November 7, 2021 for a two-day official visit to Cambodia. November 08, 2021.
During the visit, Ms. Pan will have a bilateral meeting with Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, to discuss bilateral cooperation in various fields as well as regional and international issues of common interest and concern. Including Cambodia's chairmanship of ASEAN in 2022.
With this visit, Fresh News would like to quote her profile as well as the history of Cambodia-Australia relations for further understanding.
I. Curriculum vitae of Ms. Mary Pann, Australian Chief of Mission
When the alliance between the Liberal Party and the Nationalist Party won the general election in 2013. Ms. Pence has been named Minister of Service in the government of Tony Abbott. After Malcolm Thornbull defeated Tony Abbott, Pence became Secretary of Defense in September 2015. She is the second woman to hold this position.
In August 2018, Malcolm Thornebull was replaced by Scott Morrison as chairman of the Liberal Party and prime minister. Foreign Minister Julie Bishop, who failed to run for president of the Liberal Party, has resigned.
Morrison later decided to nominate Mary Penn as Secretary of State to replace Bishop in the new government. The move makes Ms. Penn the second woman to hold the position as well. Following the Morrison administration's re-election in 2019, she was reappointed as Minister of Women's Affairs.
II. History of Cambodia-Australia relations
Bilateral relations: Australia and Cambodia began to establish diplomatic relations in 1952. Australia provided strong support for Cambodia's path to peace in the late 1980s and early 1990s. These include playing a leading role in the United Nations Transitional Authority in Cambodia (UNTAC). Today, Australia remains firmly committed to Cambodia's development.
This diplomatic relationship allows Australia and Cambodia to work together on issues that are important to both countries and the region as a whole, and it supports improvements that benefit the Cambodian people. Australia and Cambodia are also joint members of regional and international forums such as the East Asia Summit, the ASEAN Regional Forum and the Asia-Europe Summit.
Australia has supported the ECCC in investigating and prosecuting Khmer Rouge leaders. From 2004 to 2020, Australia contributed $ 44.05 million to the hybrid tribunal.
* Defense and Security:
Australia and Cambodia work closely together to combat smuggling and human trafficking, illegal immigration, child sex tourism, drug trafficking, fraud and terrorism. At the same time, Australia has a defense cooperation program under the goal of helping Cambodia develop a modern and effective defense force to contribute to maintaining stability and security in the region.
* People-to-people connection:
Since 2014, more than 1,900 Australian students have come to study and train in Cambodia under the NCP project, while the Cambodian government and the private sector in Cambodia have given their full support to the project.
Since 1994, more than 850 Cambodians have studied in Australia through the Australian Scholarship Program. In 2019, 3,000 Cambodians enrolled in Australian educational institutions, making Australia the most popular English language destination for Cambodian students. In total, since 2002, more than 17,000 Cambodians have studied in Australia.
Trade and Investment:
During the crisis, COVID-19 Australia assisted Cambodia through a development cooperation program aimed at social protection, economic recovery and vaccination support.
As a developing country, Cambodian products are tax-exempt when imported to Australia. Cambodia and Australia have an agreement to open bilateral markets, which was achieved during Cambodia's accession to the World Trade Organization in October 2004.
The Australian Chamber of Commerce in Cambodia was established in 1995 to promote the interests of the Australian business community in Cambodia. Australian investment in Cambodia focuses on education, food and beverage, financial services, mining-resources, hospitality, and clothing.
On the morning of November 7, 2021, Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, the wife of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, led his wife to harvest rice in Kraing Thnong village, Kraing Thnong commune, Bati district. Takeo Province.
In the fields, golden ripe rice falls flat on top of each other, which shows the optimal yield for the rice crop of the farmers. Rice is an important crop and has been associated with the life as well as the livelihood of Cambodian farmers since ancient times. This means that if the rice yield is good, it will also help improve the lives of farmers.
The cool breeze under the light of the sun at dawn shines with golden light reflecting on the smiling shoulders of Samdech Kittiprittbandit when he sees the wonderful rice fields in the fields. The smile is a happy expression of remembrance when reminiscing about the life of a peasant family in the past and an expression of joy with the farmers who get good yields for dependence in life.
With a bright smile, Samdech Kittipritthbandit and his wife and other leaders went together to harvest many rice fields happily and never seemed tired. Although Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen is the wife of the nation's top leader, Samdech Chea Kol Srey has a deep passion for life as a farmer who loves to grow vegetables, fruits and grow rice.
