(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian and Thai National Anti-Drug Authority continues to work together to fight drug trafficking across borders.
The joint statement of the two countries' authorities was made during a meeting held on the evening of November 5, 2021 between General Meas Virith, Secretary-General of the National Authority for Combating Drugs and Mr. Vichai Jai Mong Khon. The Secretary-General of the Thai Drug Control Council, with the participation of representatives of relevant units of drug control bodies in both Cambodia and Thailand, participated directly and online.
General Meas Virith said, "After the meeting, the two sides agreed to continue to help each other by exchanging information, monitoring traffic, criminal networks and money laundering to strengthen efficiency." "Cooperation in the fight against drugs, especially the suppression of cross-border drug trafficking."
General Meas Virith continued that the problem of cross-border drug trafficking is still a serious situation that requires our two countries to work together to prevent and crack down on the roots of criminals, organized organizations for the safety, security and well-being of the people. Of our two nations.
General Meas Virith confirmed that in the face of the global situation facing the problem of Kovid 19 epidemic, the borders of the countries of the world have been tightly closed, although it is difficult to transport drugs through, but the production of drugs by criminals continues. Intense action, including stealth methods, becomes more flexible and complex.
The high interest rates due to their long-term lending are expected to earn higher interest rates while many people still need to borrow money.
Regarding the provision of deposit rates in Cambodia, Kaing Tong Ngy, a spokesman for the Cambodian Microfinance Association, told Fresh News on November 5 that as the Cambodian economy is growing, lending to businesses has increased. Increased interest rates make it possible for banks or financial institutions to offer high interest rates, thus enabling them to offer higher interest rates to depositors.
He said that now some financial institutions can dare to offer high interest rates up to 9.25% per annum for deposits in both KHR and USD for a five-year deposit.
"In Cambodia, there is a fair amount of competition on interest rates, so it benefits those who have money left over, and we see that during the spread of Kovid 19, despite business problems, "Anyway, the amount of deposits has increased."
According to the spokesperson of the Cambodian Microfinance Association, from quarter to quarter, deposits in Cambodia increased by around 10%. According to the report of the National Bank of Cambodia, as of the end of September 2021, the amount of deposits in commercial banks and microfinance institutions received 37.33 billion US dollars, receiving from 12.05 million customer accounts.
