Senior Minister Sun Chanthol: Electric vehicles play an important role in reducing carbon emissions
(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, stated that electric vehicles play an important role in reducing carbon emissions into the atmosphere, which can harm the environment.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol stated this on November 4, 2021, on the occasion of a courtesy call from Ms. Tina Red Shaw, Ambassador of the United Kingdom to Cambodia and the accompanying delegation. Ministry leaders and other relevant professional officials at the Ministry of Public Works.
The Australian Ambassador also stated that the Australian Government has been actively supporting the renewable energy development strategy as part of the energy development plan for Cambodia in collaboration with the private sector, and preliminary studies have shown that Cambodia has a lot of solar and wind energy, which is a potential source of renewable energy and can also guarantee cost-effectiveness.
At the same time, Mr. Keo Rattanak also expressed optimism about the cooperation between the electricity supply and the electricity grid in the future. With the smart policy and commitment of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Cambodia currently has 9% renewable energy in the electricity distribution network of Cambodia and in the future will increase to 10% while the electricity supply is powered by renewable energy. At the global level, on average, about 4% to 5% are in good condition.
It should be noted that electric cars help clean the environment more efficiently than gasoline cars and can help reduce air pollution in cities such as Phnom Penh, because this type of car does not use gasoline in its operation. Electric cars also help reduce carbon emissions into the atmosphere, even if the grid is run on coal.
