US President Joe Biden balances pressure on Iran as nuclear talks resume
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Joe Biden is weighing the balance between threats and concessions to persuade Iran to resume its 2015 nuclear deal as nuclear talks resume in November. This was released by the AFP news agency on Friday, November 5, 2021.
Iran agrees to resume talks with Western powers in Vienna, Austria, on November 29, after spending five months rescuing the 2015 nuclear deal. Restrictions on Tehran's uranium enrichment in exchange for easing economic sanctions
But now Iran is reportedly continuing to increase uranium enrichment beyond what is stipulated under the agreement, while some Western nations backing the 2015 nuclear deal have also warned Iran that it would Turns out to be worthless if Iran continues to increase uranium enrichment.
The administration of President Joe Biden has agreed to attend an indirect meeting with Iran in Vienna with the intention of reviving the 2015 Iran nuclear deal, which former President Donald Trump pulled out of the United States. Trump has imposed economic sanctions on Iran, including unilateral sanctions on oil exports, forcing Iran to suspend implementation of the agreement.
