Taliban bans use of US dollar in economy as it runs out of cash
Kabul: The Taliban government of Afghanistan has banned the use of foreign currency in the country, a shocking decision that could put further strain on the country's struggling economy. According to Bloomberg News.
The move comes as the Taliban are pushing for the release of billions of dollars in foreign reserves that have been frozen by the United States and its Western allies since the Taliban came to power last year. August. Without these reserves, Afghanistan's central bank has struggled to keep the dollar flowing into the economy.
"The use of foreign currency has a negative impact on the foreign economy," the spokesman said in a statement. "Violators of this decision will be punished according to the law."
It is unclear how the Taliban government will implement this decision, as Afghanistan's economy has relied on the US dollar since the US invasion in 2001 following the September 11 events. . Two-thirds of bank deposits in Afghanistan and half of the country's national loans are in dollars.
Afghanistan's Afghan currency has continued to depreciate against the US dollar since the Taliban came to power. The currency fell to an all-time low of 91.03 Afghani to the US dollar on Wednesday. According to Bloomberg.
