Phnom Penh Municipal Court: Court thoroughly judges juvenile interests before sentencing Kak Sovanchhay
(Phnom Penh): A spokesman for the Phnom Penh Municipal Court confirmed that the conviction of Kak Sovanchhay was made with careful consideration of the juvenile interests and that his sentence was commuted. This is in stark contrast to the views of human rights groups.
Kak Sovanchhay, a 17-year-old Cambodian man, the son of a former CNRP activist, was charged with inciting serious social unrest and insulting crimes committed in Cambodia in 2011. And June 23, 2021. The allegations came after the young man wrote several messages on Facebook and telegram.
On November 1, 2021, a judge of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court sentenced him to eight months in prison, but sentenced him to four and a half months in prison, with the remainder of his sentence suspended, requiring the prisoner to comply with inspection measures. Some. He will be released from prison on November 8.
Following this decision, there was some criticism from civil society organizations on human rights as well as the guardian of this young man.
The spokesman added that the man was instructed to provide education without charge in court proceedings, as he was believed to be a minor who committed the crime for the first time, but he still did not change his ways. Law for this second case.
In particular, the claim that this person has autism is a personal claim without showing proof, no medical certificate, previous medical records and no forensic certificate. The court examined the physical condition and inquired about the general understanding of the person, that he was normal like other children, without any abnormalities as mentioned by the family.
Director of Disability Foundation inspected the construction and renovation of Siem Reap Rehabilitation Center
(Siem Reap): Mr. Sim Sothoun, Director of the Disability Foundation, led a delegation of the Disability Foundation to inspect the progress of the construction of a new building and renovation of the Siem Reap Provincial Rehabilitation Center on November 4, 2021.
According to the report of Mr. Chou Bunnarith, Branch Manager of Siem Reap Disability Foundation, Siem Reap is one of the 19 worst affected provinces with Kovid, which mainly affects the provision of rehabilitation services to the disabled. .
On behalf of the officials of all centers and people with disabilities in Siem Reap, Mr. Chou Bunnarith would like to thank the Director for encouraging and supporting the construction project in order to improve the quality of services in Siem Reap. Up.
Speaking at the inspection, Mr. Sim Sothoun, Director of the Disability Foundation, said that considering the needs of the Siem Reap Provincial Rehabilitation Center, the Disability Foundation decided to provide the construction of a 12-meter by 17-meter-high building. One floor has 5 rooms.
Together with the Disability Fund, they also renovated an administrative building, a production building, a living room and an office building, a warehouse, and renovated a public square, as well as demolished and rebuilt the front and back fences of the center.
