(Pursat): Invasion of state-owned land in Thmor Da area, Veal Veng district, Pursat province, has resumed after Mao Thunin was relocated to Kampot province. Pursat provincial authorities have warned of legal action against those attempting to illegally occupy state-owned land.
The authorities' warning came after a man named Ray Kan alias Vat repeatedly tried to encroach on state-owned land at the ivory point in Ekpheap village, Thmor Da commune, Veal Veng district.
Thmor Da is a remote area in the past that no one misses because it is far from the province with no way to travel. In 2010, the Royal Government handed over the land in the rocky area to a private company to develop agro-industrial crops and become a special economic zone.
After the Royal Government completed the construction of National Road 55, the rocky area is no longer isolated as in the past and has become the most attractive area in Pursat province. This has also led to many settlements and settlements by people from various areas. At the same time, land disputes in the area also erupted.
Ray Kan, a senior Khmer Rouge soldier, claimed that he had owned land at the site since 1996, when he was a Khmer Rouge soldier in an area controlled by the Khmer Rouge.
Contrary to Ray Kan's claims, authorities confirmed that Ray Kan did not have land at the site. Mr. Khoy Rida, Deputy Governor of Pursat Province, said that through the Royal Government's Policy 001 in 2012, the youth group has already measured and distributed the land to the people. There are also cuts from economic land concessions that the Royal Government has given to private investment companies.
Khoy Rida claimed that at the time of the survey, there was no name for the person who owned the land in the area.
In connection with the land dispute in this area, on October 26, 2020, Mr. Mao Thunin, when he was the Governor of Pursat Province, also set up a provincial working group to prevent the encroachment on this state land, with him as the chairman.
Through this hot class, the activity of encroaching on the land at the above location is also calm. But after Mao Thunin moved out of Pursat, activities were resumed.
Mr. Khoy Rida confirmed that the provincial authorities will continue to strictly abide by the decisions that are still valid in the fight against and prevent encroachment on illegal land ownership.
The deputy governor of Pursat province also claimed that the authorities would take legal action against those who encroached on state-owned land. The deputy governor considered these actions to be a malicious intent on land issues in the province, which has been quiet for a long time. Chinese Embassy in Cambodia Continues to Donate 10,000 NK95 Masks and Other Materials to Cambodia-China Friendship Youth Volunteer Doctors
