Ministry of Justice Spokesman: Cambodian Law Enforcement on Individuals Is Not a Human Rights Violation
The Justice Department spokesman's statement came after the US Ambassador to Cambodia took to Twitter on November 2, 202 to express his dissatisfaction with the sentencing of the group activists. An opponent is charged with incitement to commit a human rights violation.
At this point, Mr. Jean Malin emphasized the legal aspect that based on the national law of Cambodia, minors aged 14 years and older must be criminally responsible for the crimes they have committed, while the punishment must be punished. Some legal benefits, such as a reduction of the sentence to half of the maximum sentence for the crime committed and the suspension, etc.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Justice stated: "The application of the law through the judiciary and based on the national legal procedures in force is not a violation of human rights, but the promotion of human rights and the rule of law in society. Democracy. "On the contrary, the use of violence and extrajudicial killings of suspects on the basis of color is a serious and serious violation of human rights in connection with racism."
Please be informed that the Phnom Penh Municipal Court on November 1, 2021 sentenced the accused Kak Sovanchhay to 8 months imprisonment, but to serve only 4 months and 15 days, with the remaining sentence suspended. He was charged with inciting and insulting the government. The sentencing was announced by Trial Judge Oung Vuthea.
