About 40 km from Phnom Penh, if we take National Road 2, turn into the road to Phnom Mao resort, about 1 km on the right we will see a large farm. This place is a large dairy farm in Cambodia with a total area of 300 hectares in the Tamao Valley, located in Ophasaing village, Kandeung commune, Bati district, Takeo province.
Kiri Sour Farm was established by Cambodian investors in collaboration with Israel with an initial investment of nearly $ 20 million. Khmer Fresh Milk Kiri Sour Kork Dairy Farm, launched in 2019, is considered to be the largest and most modern farm in Cambodia, using the latest Israeli technology for raising, growing grass and fodder production. Which makes the farm's cows capable of producing more milk.
The other 100 hectares are used for cattle barns and for farm production. Currently, Kiri Sour Farm can produce fresh milk with a total production volume of more than 10,000 liters to supply the local market in Phnom Penh and some provinces.
In addition to being affordable, Kiri Sour Fresh Milk is made to a high standard of hygiene and does not use any preservatives, taking just one hour to deliver to the market. By 2022, the farm will increase the number of cows that can produce 1,000 cows to reach 20,000 liters of milk per day.
Mr. Chhor Rithy said that Kiri Sour milk has entered the first test market since September 2020 and will be officially launched during March 2021, which in the first nine months captured about 10% market share. And will be in the Top 3 for the fresh milk market in Cambodia.
He added that Kiri Sour will try to expand production to supply the domestic market, contributing to reducing the import of fresh milk from abroad. In addition to producing fresh milk for sale on the market, the farm also produces yogurt and butter, and in the future the farm will also produce some dairy products.
Despite importing technology from Israel, almost 90% of the farm's staff are Cambodians, who live around Phnom Tamao, with the aim of providing new knowledge and techniques on cattle breeding. Milk, weeding and keeping fodder for cows in a modern way for Cambodians. Of the more than 100 employees, only six are foreign.
He encourages all Cambodians to drink plenty of fresh milk to get healthy, strong bones and reduce stress.
