Scientists and UN officials have warned that if governments do not take immediate action to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, the world could face a catastrophic climate change. Such as sea level rise, heat waves, floods and many other crises.
The Paris Agreement aims to keep temperatures below 2 degrees Celsius, 1.5 degrees Celsius, just before the Industrial Revolution. But global warming has already risen to 1.1 degrees Celsius, as a UN assessment released in August predicts that global warming will exceed 1.5 degrees Celsius in the next two decades. .
- Finance: Experts say finance will be a major challenge at COP26. Developing countries have demanded that rich countries unite to release $ 100 billion a year to help them reduce greenhouse gas emissions and learn to adapt to the effects of climate change.
Carbon Market: Guidelines for the implementation of the Paris Agreement are agreed upon by all Member States, except for Article 6. This article sets out how to develop and implement international carbon markets that allow for the trading of carbon credits or the reduction of greenhouse gas emissions.
Coal: British Prime Minister Boris Johnson has called on all rich countries to stop using coal, a major source of greenhouse gas emissions, by 2030 and for other countries by 2040. But talks on "coal" continue to be controversial and controversial, with the G7 countries not agreeing on a specific date for cessation of coal use in early 2021.
Methane: Government leaders from around the world will formally pledge to reduce "methane emissions" by at least 30 percent by 2030, a goal the United States and the European Union have revealed. Last September as well.
