WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Joe Biden has told Southeast Asian leaders that the United States will stand with the region to defend maritime freedom and democracy, and he called China's actions in Taiwan "a step in the right direction." Coercion ”and threatens peace and stability. This is according to the release by CNA on the morning of Thursday, October 28, 2021.
"The United States has a strong commitment to Taiwan," Biden reiterated. "We are deeply concerned about China's coercive actions ... they are threatening regional peace and stability."
Southeast Asia is seen as becoming a strategic battleground between the United States and China due to sensitive issues in the South China Sea and Taiwan. Last week, Zhou Biden openly defended Taiwan in the event of an attack from China, which is further straining cross-strait relations.
After Zhou Biden's remarks to ASEAN countries and direct attacks on China, Chinese Premier Li Keqiang, through meetings with the 10 ASEAN countries and other regional partners, responded: "Keeping peace, stability, freedom of navigation and navigation in the South China Sea is in everyone's interest. "The South China Sea is our common home."
On Wednesday, the embassy in Washington issued a statement stating that US efforts to strengthen and support the "Taiwan independence movement" were futile and would fail as attempted. Washington to create a "one China, one Taiwan" or "two China" in the United Nations (UN) 50 years ago.
The Chinese Embassy urges the United States to "stop formal relations with Taiwan, stop making irresponsible and erroneous remarks, and take concrete steps to improve the overall situation of Sino-US relations and peace and stability across the Straits." "
