WTO agrees to set up China-Australia alcohol tax settlement working group
GENEVA: The World Trade Organization (WTO) has said it has agreed to set up a task force to look into China's tariffs on wine imported from Australia. According to Reuters.
The alcohol tax dispute between the two countries is one of many that has affected the relations between the two major economies.
Reuters reports that the WTO, a global trade dispute settlement body, has agreed to Australia's second request after the first one was blocked by China in September.
China has responded by lifting tariffs on Australian wine and wheat and restricting imports of Australian beef, charcoal and grapes, a move that the United States has described as "economic repression." .
In an explanation to the WTO, the Australian delegation expressed disappointment that China had not taken strong action to respond to its demands and that Australia was still open to further discussions with China. Solve this problem.
"China regrets that Australia has decided to continue its request for a working group to resolve the dispute," the Chinese delegation said in a statement sent to Reuters.
