In an online meeting with leaders from ASEAN, Chinese Premier Li Keqiang called on ASEAN and China to further deepen economic integration between the two partners and launch a free trade partnership agreement. Regional Comprehensive Economy (RCEP) as soon as possible.
Li Keqiang also called for cooperation in areas such as climate change, science and technology and innovation, Xinhua reported.
The Nikkei Asia reported that the prime minister's remarks indicated Beijing's intention to join the CPTPP, of which Vietnam, Malaysia, Singapore and ASEAN member Brunei are members of the trade agreement. Gaining the support of these four countries for China's participation is very important for Beijing.
One of the problems China has in common with the four countries is the large number of state-owned enterprises, which are barriers to joining the FTA.
While the trade agreement includes restrictions on Vietnamese state-owned companies whose state-owned companies play a key role in the economy, some restrictions are waived. China can look at this.
A former Chinese diplomat told the Nikkei Asia that "the creation of exemptions should be considered for issues that are extremely difficult to resolve", such as state-owned companies. "The Chinese market is relevant to other countries," the former diplomat said. "It is important to demonstrate these benefits in order to benefit from the negotiations."
In the northern Lao province of Bokeo, travel between districts is banned until November 8. Local markets at high risk of COVID-19 infection have been temporarily closed while street food sales have also been suspended. Food delivery can continue as usual, but all food must come from a local green restaurant and payment must be made by bank card to avoid cash.
