Mr. Kao Kim Hourn, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister, emphasized that in this meeting, the ASEAN-Japan Heads of Government noted two documents, including: First: Progress Report on Improving the Implementation of the Vision Statement on ASEAN Friendship and Cooperation Japan, Second: Progress Report on the Implementation of the Joint Statement on ASEAN-Japan Cooperation on the ASEAN Vision on the Indo-Pacific.
Japan has been one of ASEAN's oldest dialogue partners for 48 years. At the meeting, the ASEAN Heads of Government agreed to the request of the Japanese side to hold a special summit in 2023, the 50th anniversary of the establishment of the ASEAN-Japan Dialogue Partnership.
The ASEAN Heads of Government also welcomed Japan's $ 250 billion loan and disaster response response to the 19-year-old Kovid, and welcomed a total of approximately $ 200 million in medical, equipment and technical assistance. Other for ASEAN.
At the meeting, the Prime Minister asked Japan to help build the ASEAN Community, especially the Master Plan for ASEAN Connectivity 2025, and to facilitate ASEAN integration. The ASEAN Heads of Government also requested Japan to reduce the development gap of ASEAN member countries and assist the Mekong countries.
The ASEAN Heads of Government expressed their satisfaction with the ASEAN-Japan cooperation and proposed to further promote the strategic partnership. In particular, expand cooperation in all fields, including digital, economic, social, cultural, security and other fields.
At the meeting, the Japanese Prime Minister announced that Japan will continue to support ASEAN's core role in the region, and Japan will continue to support all ASEAN projects. Finally, the heads of government of Japan and ASEAN discussed regional issues such as the South China Sea, Myanmar and the Korean Peninsula.
The Australian government on Tuesday night amended a law enacted in March 2020 to restrict international travel during the COVID-19 outbreak. This change means that from November 1, Australians who have received the full COVID-19 vaccine will be able to leave the country for the first time since March. 2020 ago.
Those who have not been vaccinated will still have to apply with the Department of Internal Affairs to leave the country and will face "strict catastrophic measures" when they return. As of Tuesday, more than 74 per cent of Australians aged 16 and over had been fully vaccinated, according to the Australian Health Authority.
