The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, and the leaders of all ASEAN countries attended and attended their annual summit, except for one of the leaders of the Union of Myanmar, to discuss the agenda: planned and Under the direct chairmanship of Sultan Haji Hassanal Bulgaria, the chairman of the meeting.
At the 38th ASEAN Summit, ASEAN leaders discussed two main agendas, namely: Building the ASEAN Community and Response to Kovid-19 Response. In particular, at the 39th ASEAN Summit, ASEAN leaders discussed two main agendas: first, external relations and the ASEAN Center, and second, the exchange of views on regional and international issues.
Like other ASEAN leaders, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, addressed the 38th and 39th ASEAN Summits, emphasizing the prolongation of the Kovid-19 epidemic. For the past two years, it has become a test of great ability to lead and develop both for ASEAN as a whole and for individual member countries.
At that important summit, ASEAN leaders agreed to issue the ASEAN Leaders' Statement on the Promotion of Multilateralism, which includes nine points:
1. Recall ASEAN's need to maintain unity, cohesion and resilience in upholding its objectives and principles as enshrined in the ASEAN Charter in support of multilateralism.
2. Reaffirm ASEAN's joint commitment to maintaining and promoting peace, security and stability in the region, as well as the peaceful settlement of disputes, including full respect for the rule of law and diplomacy without the use of intimidation; The use of force in accordance with the universally recognized principles of international law, including the 1982 United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS).
3. Strengthen ASEAN Centrality and Unity in ASEAN's constructive participation with its external partners through ASEAN-led mechanisms to build mutual trust and confidence, as well as strengthen open regional architecture. Open, transparent, environmental and based on legal measures, with ASEAN at the center.
4. Reaffirm the objectives and principles of the ASEAN Indo-Pacific Perspective (AOIP), which provide a guide for ASEAN participation in relations in the Asia-Pacific region and the Indian Ocean region.
5. Recall ASEAN's strong support for an open, transparent, non-discriminatory and rule-based multilateral trading system, including through the early entry into force of the Economic Partnership Agreement. Regional Corridor (RCEP) and the full implementation of the ASEAN Plus One Free Trade Agreement.
6. Promote the harmonization, complementarity and integration of inter-sectoral issues such as climate change, environmental protection and cyber security to address the common impact and consequences of interrelated challenges.
7. Strengthen cooperation towards the improvement of our global public health system and to ensure equitable access to drugs for the treatment of Kovid-19 by making Kovid-19 vaccine a commodity. Public: Global, available and affordable for the general public.
8. Encourage more cooperation in the three pillars of the ASEAN Community and for all stakeholders to continue their efforts to improve cross-sectoral and cross-pillar coordination towards achieving ASEAN Community Vision 2025 and Development of ASEAN Post-2025 Vision
