Half a century since the new China, a member of the United Nations, has been actively involved in safeguarding world peace, international order and multilateralism.
Fifty years ago, China, which had just recovered from a chronic war, re-emerged at the United Nations. A fiery red flag with five gold stars fluttered in the wind at the United Nations Headquarters in New York, showing the world that China has re-emerged on an equal footing with other nations. Member of the United Nations.
For the past 50 years, as a permanent member of the UN Security Council and under close co-operation with the United Nations, China has played an increasingly important role in promoting stability, peace, progress and prosperity. The world through its practical application.
Especially after the outbreak of Kovid 19, in addition to providing medical assistance, China has begun to consider the vaccine as a public product, becoming the first country in the world to provide Kovid 19 vaccine to foreign countries in large quantities. Most as of now.
The facts show to the whole world that China has always lived up to its promise to be a world peace builder, a contributor to global development, a supporter of international order and order, and a major supplier of public products. Exactly. Reform and opening-up policies are the first step in the new China joining the world community.
UN Secretary-General António Guterres said in a meeting with Chinese President Xi Jinping on October 25 that the Global Development Initiative outlined by Xi Jinping is important and positive. The United Nations is fully committed to promoting universal equality and balancing sustainable development, and is pleased to increase cooperation with the Chinese side in this regard.
What else will China contribute to safeguarding world peace, international order and multilateralism, bringing about the common good of all mankind? This is the expectation of all friendly countries, partners and peace lovers.
The US Embassy in Brunei told Reuters that Biden would lead a US delegation to the ASEAN-US Summit as part of this week's ASEAN Leaders' Meeting. The United States has not attended a presidential meeting since former President Donald Trump attended the 2017 ASEAN-US Summit in Manila, Philippines.
Analysts see the meeting between Biden and the 10 ASEAN leaders as a reflection of his administration's efforts to mobilize allies and allies in Asia to stem China's growing influence. At the summit with ASEAN, Biden is expected to focus on cooperation in distributing the COVID-19 vaccine, climate change, supply chain and infrastructure.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen Attends ASEAN Summit and Related Summits
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, this morning, October 26, 2021, is leading senior officials of the Royal Government of Cambodia to attend the ASEAN Summit and related summits via video.
The ASEAN Summit and related summits in 2021 will be hosted by Brunei, but due to the Kovid 19 crisis, all the leaders of the countries concerned could not attend in person and had to be replaced by video.
Senior government officials accompanying Samdech Techo to these meetings include: Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, Dr. Hang Chuon Naron, Minister of Education, Youth and Sports as well as some senior officials of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
Please note that the ASEAN Summit and related summits will last for 3 days from October 26 to 28. On the last day, Samdech Techo Prime Minister will intervene in the closing ceremony after Brunei handed over the chairmanship of ASEAN 2022 to Cambodia.
The negotiation of the CKFTA agreement was initiated by the Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen during the state visit of the President of the Republic of Korea MOON Jae-In to the Kingdom of Cambodia in March 2019 to strengthen and Expand economic and trade ties between the two countries.
Pursuant to the mandate given by the leaders of the two countries, the Ministers of Commerce of the two countries announced the start of the joint study on November 25, 2019 in Busan, Republic of Korea.
The study shows positive results for both countries, strengthening economic partnership and expanding bilateral trade liberalization, mutually beneficial, aimed at enhancing the economic and well-being of the people and business of the two countries. Both countries.
The CKFTA agreement was officially opened for talks on July 9, 2020 via video by the two countries' trade ministers, and went through a total of 12 rounds of negotiations before reaching its signing. This reflects the strong commitment of both sides to overcome all obstacles, despite the difficult conditions that caused Kovid-19, to achieve this important historical achievement.
In addition, the agreement will boost export growth, increase gross domestic product and increase employment opportunities, as well as help the two economies recover more quickly from Kovid-19 by expanding Add market liberalization in addition to the ASEAN-Korea Free Trade Agreement (AKFTA) and the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP).
