(Phnom Penh): The Paris Peace Agreement, an agreement to end the political conflict in Cambodia, is 30 years old, on October 23, 2021 tomorrow. The establishment of this agreement was highly valued by Dr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, on the will of the Cambodian side, especially His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk, who represents the tripartite resistance movement, and Samdech Techo Hun Sen, representing the government. The state of Cambodia at that time.
Although this agreement has been in force for 30 years, there are still various opinions and interpretations of the reasons for this agreement in the Cambodian society, politics and public opinion.
On the morning of October 22, 2021, the Royal Academy of Cambodia, a research institution in Cambodia, organized a roundtable discussion on the Paris Peace Agreement with the aim of raising awareness and providing items for consideration by the Cambodian people. The forum also invited speakers from civil society organizations, social analysts, political party representatives and researchers.
Speakers at the forum included: Dr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, Dr. Kin Phea, President of the Institute of International Relations of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, Ms. Chak Sopheap, Executive Director of the Cambodian Center for Human Rights, Mr. Ou Chanrath, Founder of the Cambodian Reform Party, Dr. Meas Ny Neak Analyze and monitor social development and Mr. Yang Saing Koma, Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Local Democrat Party.
Through this forum, all speakers raised the aspects that led to the Paris Peace Agreement, the achievements arising from the agreement, the validity, the challenges and the recommendations for further implementation.
All the speakers expressed similar views, applauding the agreement, which led to Cambodia ending the flames of war and building the country. But the speakers seem to have different views around the characters, leading to an agreement, the risks and validity of this agreement.
Representatives of civil society organizations such as Ms. Chak Sopheap, political party representative Mr. Yang Saing Komar, and social observer Mr. Meas Nyhak have valued the superpower as a key contributor to the establishment of the Paris Peace Accords. They also felt that the agreement was still valid and valid for Cambodia.
Sok Touch argued that some countries, even if the superpowers had collapsed, could not put out the flames of war, such as Afghanistan, Angola and Nigeria.
"This Paris Agreement was born out of the will of the Khmer people, but we say it is the weakness of the superpowers," he said. But Khmer is the will, and those who do that, do not forget the man Nong is Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
In addition to presenting the key players in bringing about the Paris Agreement, Dr. Sok Touch also raised the issue of whether the Paris Peace Agreement has expired or is still valid.
For Dr. Sok Touch, the Paris Peace Accords were a transitional agreement that expired after Cambodia established its constitution and government after the 1993 elections.
Mr. Sok Touch also mentioned that the Thai invasion of the Temple of Preah Vihear and the Lao invasion of Stung Treng are all evidence that the Paris Peace Agreement is no longer valid because it is still valid. These two countries did not invade Cambodia like this. In the case of the Thai invasion in 2008, the United Nations and ASEAN did not suffer from seeking Cambodian intervention.
