Myanmar has been in turmoil since a coup overthrew Aung San Suu Kyi's government in February 2021. So far, human rights watchdogs in the Southeast Asian country have claimed that more than 1,100 people have been killed and more than 8,000 arrested in crackdowns on opposition movements across the country. Especially in Rangoon, Mandalay and Nay Pyi Taw.
Earlier Monday, Myanmar authorities announced that they would release more than 5,000 people during a three-day Buddhist festival. This has prompted families with relatives in custody to gather in front of the jail, hoping to reunite with their loved ones who will be released.
Although the Myanmar military claims that more than 5,000 people have been released, the actual number of prisoners released across the country remains difficult to verify, and many more are still being released. After they signed a contract never to make such a mistake again.
However, according to a report from the Association for Assistance to Political Prisoners in Myanmar (AAPP), at least 110 of those pardoned have been repatriated. According to the latest report, Myanmar authorities released more than 2,000 anti-coup protesters from prisons across the country in June, including journalists critical of the military government.
