Indonesia needs $ 200 billion a year by 2030 to achieve zero carbon emissions
This considerable amount of money reflects the need for large-scale financing in this coal-dependent country.
A report released by the Indonesian Ministry of National Development Planning says a substantial amount of financing will be needed to transform Indonesia into a carbon-free country by 2060 through investment exchanges. Existing and new financial sources.
Funding for the change could come from the abolition of fuel subsidies and carbon offsets, which could save money and generate about $ 2.2 billion in new revenue, the report said. Percentage of GDP in 2030. Indonesia recently approved tax regulatory reforms, launching a carbon tax exemption scheme.
With "appropriate regulatory reforms and risk mitigation measures such as ensuring joint operations and public-private partnerships," Indonesia could also facilitate private investment, especially this decade and the next decade, when the ministry said. Indonesia's national development plan expects investment to reach record highs.
