Vendors abusing street food in Ponley Market, Kampong Chhnang Province, residents asked the authorities to facilitate traffic for fear of causing accidents
(Kampong Chhnang): Passengers in all kinds of vehicles in Boribo district, Kampong Chhnang province have expressed their grievances and worries for fear of causing accidents after vendors were selling illegally on the road in Ponley market, causing congestion every morning. Market tax collectors have also contributed to traffic congestion by using the road as a place to send motorbikes to collect more money.
Motorcyclists also said that if there is an accident, it could be caused by the wheels of the truck grinding on the goods sold on the road or causing harm to vendors and shoppers on the road. This is because the placement of goods on public roads does not seem to have a supervisor and order.
Many travelers have requested the Boribo district authorities to help check the traffic at this market to avoid traffic jams every morning, and if this continues, one day it may cause another accident. Eventually, too.
Mr. Phal Sopheap said that in the past, commune and district authorities have also disseminated educational advice to traders who sell goods and vegetables on the road. He said that if there is a traffic accident, please do not blame the authorities because the authorities do not allow sales on this road.
The district authority will further advise market tax collectors and shopkeepers on this road to return to the concrete road designated by the district authority and to avoid possible traffic accidents. Due to such anarchic trade as well.
(Koh Kong): The cemetery of 447 war veterans who lost their lives during the war was inaugurated on February 23, 2021 under the presidency of Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of National Defense. National team visits Koh Kong province.
As of October 20, 2021, the construction and repair team of the cemetery area of 447 war veterans, about 75% completed. This was confirmed to Fresh News by General Yon Min, Secretary of State and Chairman of the Construction Commission.
"According to the inspection process, the construction of the veterans 'graves so far has been about 75 percent of the total veterans' graves," said General Min. "There are three obstacles: too much rain, second, the epidemic of Kovid 19, which makes the construction team less concentrated, and third: the budget is still lacking!"
At the same time, General Yon Min said that despite some problems, the construction team is still continuing its work to complete the construction of the veterans' grave as planned.
According to General Yon Min, the construction and renovation of the Veterans' Cemetery is more than 9,500 square meters in Village 3, Sangkat Smach Meanchey, Khemarak Phoumin Municipality, Koh Kong Province. The cemetery has a coffin, a grave for each warrior, a funeral home, a charitable name and a parking lot.
General Yon Min said that the construction and renovation of the Veterans Tomb is now in retaliation for the soldiers who sacrificed their lives during the war for the sake of territorial integrity, defense of the motherland and protection of life. The life of the people to bring peace to the nation and the people, regardless of the cost of their lives.
In addition, the General continued that the implementation of the recommendations of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and implemented under the guidance of Samdech Pichey Sena Tea Banh, the Ministry of National Defense will build this area to be appropriate and become a grateful area. Concerns: Veterans' corpses.
The General Secretary of State emphasized that the cemetery for veterans should be built in the Khmer style. In addition, a team of experts studied and improved the style of Angkor, and the history of this site placed the bodies of soldiers who sacrificed their lives for the integrity of the territory from July 1979 to 1998.
