The OECD says that in order to maintain public services and benefits while maintaining a stable debt environment, governments will need to increase revenue by nearly 8 percent of gross domestic product (GDP). In some countries, including France and Japan, the magnitude of the impact will be more than 10 percent of GDP, and this figure does not include recent costs such as climate change adaptation costs.
"Global trends such as an aging population and rising service prices will continue to put pressure on government budgets," the OECD said in its policy document. "The fiscal pressure that comes from this long-term trend is stronger than the public pollution caused by Kovid-19."
The OECD said countries did not need to raise taxes to meet these challenges, but instead called for reforms to increase employment and increase retirement age.
According to the Paris-based organization France, action in both areas could halve the projected fiscal pressure by 2060 in a country under moderate pressure.
The Washington-based fund now expects the Asia-Pacific economy to grow 6.5 percent this year, down 1.1 percentage points from the forecast, Bloomberg reported. Economic outlook in April, but with this growth, the Asia-Pacific region remains the fastest growing region in the world.
The region's economy is expected to grow 4.9 percent in 2022, 0.4 percentage points higher than the previous estimate, according to the IMF.
China's economic forecast fell to 8 percent from a previous estimate of 8.4 percent due to ongoing virus outbreaks, tightening of fiscal policy and problems in the real estate sector.
