Rising fuel retail prices have caused people to complain, with some commenting that the rise is hurting their livelihoods.
Regarding the price increase, Mr. Pen Sovicheat, Undersecretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, said that the increase depends on the increase in oil prices in the international market and Cambodia depends on oil imports.
Mr. Pen Sovichet added that the administration has stabilized this growth by maintaining the same amount of taxes and subsidies by continuing to subsidize 4 cents per liter to prevent the increase as much as possible. Affect the livelihood of the people.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce pointed out that OPEC, a group of oil-producing countries, had recently forecast lower demand and now demand was higher, so global oil prices are rising. Up.
Mr. Pen Sovicheat said that the demand for oil has increased due to the reopening of travel in each country. Obviously, Cambodia is a country in the developed region, there are factories, enterprises are still open, etc. Cambodia is considering the possibility of further high growth, but does not dare to promise how oil prices will be set.
He expects OPEC to increase production and international oil prices to fall. Cambodia will strive to maintain the import of oil for use in the market to meet the needs of people to have access to daily life.
"It's not that the state is indifferent, it is taking concrete steps to analyze international values," Pen Sovichet said. "If the import price is too high, we will reconsider the price of oil."
