Readers' Perspectives: Cambodia's Journey Towards Reconciliation, Struggle and Pain
Between 1975 and 1979, Cambodia was under the control of the Khmer Rouge communist movement, which created unpredictable fears and committed massacres. The goal of the Khmer Rouge regime was to destroy the nation and begin its "zero year", known worldwide as the "Killing Fields." In less than four years, the Khmer Rouge regime killed more than two million Cambodians.
In 1979, for example, Cambodia established a "People's Revolutionary Court" that "tried" the Khmer Rouge leaders in absentia. While efforts to bring justice to the Khmer Rouge regime were justified, they did not stand up to internationally recognized standards of justice, and this effort was not possible at the time. The nation is still at war.
The Documentation Center of Cambodia is the largest archive of documents related to the Khmer Rouge regime in the region. DC-Cam plays an important role in promoting the truth-seeking and justice process, not just in the courtroom, but throughout Cambodia.
Following the establishment of the Khmer Rouge Tribunal, Cambodia not only supported the search for justice at the tribunal through the provision of documents, exhibits and interviews, but also the achievement of justice in education, memory, social services and Other support for survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime.
Cambodia has grown significantly since 1979, but Cambodia has gone through a lot of struggle and pain to get to this point. However, Cambodia has never hesitated. Today, more than 5 million survivors of the Khmer Rouge, including the perpetrators, live to tell their stories during the Khmer Rouge regime.
Cambodia's journey to reconciliation is not over and will never end. Preventing restraint requires a strong commitment to remembering the past.
